  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2509.20641v2</id>
    <title>Investigating Modality Contribution in Audio LLMs for Music</title>
    <updated>2026-02-02T15:29:03Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.20641v2" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.20641v2" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Audio Large Language Models (Audio LLMs) enable human-like conversation about music, yet it is unclear if they are truly listening to the audio or just using textual reasoning, as recent benchmarks suggest. This paper investigates this issue by quantifying the contribution of each modality to a model's output. We adapt the MM-SHAP framework, a performance-agnostic score based on Shapley values that quantifies the relative contribution of each modality to a model's prediction. We evaluate two models on the MuChoMusic benchmark and find that the model with higher accuracy relies more on text to answer questions, but further inspection shows that even if the overall audio contribution is low, models can successfully localize key sound events, suggesting that audio is not entirely ignored. Our study is the first application of MM-SHAP to Audio LLMs and we hope it will serve as a foundational step for future research in explainable AI and audio.</summary>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.SD" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2025-09-25T00:56:35Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>5 pages, 2 figures, accepted at ICASSP 2026</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.LG"/>
    <author>
      <name>Giovana Morais</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Magdalena Fuentes</name>
    </author>
    <arxiv:doi>10.1109/ICASSP55912.2026.11463350</arxiv:doi>
    <link rel="related" href="https://doi.org/10.1109/ICASSP55912.2026.11463350" title="doi"/>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2509.20645v4</id>
    <title>Anticipatory Evaluation of Language Models: Predicting LLM Benchmark Scores from Descriptions</title>
    <updated>2026-10-13T18:26:56Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.20645v4" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.20645v4" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Modern ML research increasingly faces an evaluation bottleneck: building benchmarks and running experiments is costly, slowing iteration. We ask how much can be inferred about evaluation outcomes before experiments, given only a natural-language description of the task and setup. We call this setting anticipatory evaluation. We introduce EVALCAST, a literature-grounded resource of 2,290 description-performance pairs from 1,519 arXiv papers, including a test set of papers published after the model's knowledge cutoff. We evaluate two tasks: predicting absolute performance through regression and comparing benchmark difficulty through pairwise prediction. Experiments with frontier LLMs show that accurate regression remains challenging, while pairwise predictions can recover relative performance ordering under matched settings. Comparable performance on post-cutoff and streaming evaluations suggests that the observed signal is not solely attributable to previously reported results. Overall, EVALCAST provides a resource and evaluation framework for studying anticipatory evaluation and its potential use in prioritizing benchmarks.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2025-09-25T01:02:27Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>29 pages, 6 figures</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CL"/>
    <author>
      <name>Jungsoo Park</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Ethan Mendes</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Gabriel Stanovsky</name>
    </author>
    <author>
